  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1104 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nUpper low now over the northern Rockies with main trough moving \nthrough New Mexico this morning. This has flushed the layered \nmoisture well east of our area. Dewpoints, though still high this \nmorning, should drop this afternoon. Model dewpoints this afternoon \nshow upper 30s north to lower 40s south. Hence, don't expect many, \nif any, thunderstorms today. Mountains still have a non-zero chance \nbut not enough to mention in weather grids. Monday looks to be about \nthe same as next upper low moves on to SoCal and shortwave ridge \nahead of the low moves over our area. Moisture ticks up slightly so \njust a slight bump up in a chance of an isolated thunderstorm.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...SoCal low drifts over the Great Basin \nwith the trough extending south-southwest over SoCal. This will \nbegin rapidly drawing up sub-tropical moisture with the help of weak \nwave aloft and increasing strength of the sub-tropical jet. Expect \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms for this period, with heavy \nrainfall likely, along with flash flood potential. By Wednesday \nmorning PWs approach 1.50 inches, which is close to record highs for \nthat date. PWs do lower some, down to 1.0 to 1.2 inches rest of this \nperiod. \n\nFriday through Sunday...models begin to diverge. Both GFS/ECMWF show \nsub-tropical high strengthening over south Texas, and possibly \nreducing moisture tap over our area. GFS is especially bearish on \nprecip chances. In a further fit of divergence, ECMWF still moves \ntropical storm remnants (Polo?) up over our area Sunday night and \nMonday for another shot of heavy rain. These are yesterday's/last \nnight's model runs so hopefully this morning's runs can unite in \ntheir solutions a little better.",
